**Dark mode in CSS** usually means giving your page two color schemes and switching between them based on the user's system setting (or a toggle).

### 1. Define colors as variables

```css
:root {
  --bg: #ffffff;
  --text: #1a1a1a;
  --accent: #2563eb;
}

body {
  background: var(--bg);
  color: var(--text);
}
```

### 2. Override them when the OS is in dark mode

```css
@media (prefers-color-scheme: dark) {
  :root {
    --bg: #121212;
    --text: #e6e6e6;
    --accent: #60a5fa;
  }
}
```

`prefers-color-scheme` reads the user's system preference, so the page follows it automatically.

### 3. Optional: a manual toggle

```css
[data-theme="dark"] {
  --bg: #121212;
  --text: #e6e6e6;
}
```

```js
document.documentElement.dataset.theme = "dark";
```

### Tips
- Add `color-scheme: light dark;` on `:root` so form controls and scrollbars match.
- Avoid pure black (`#000`) — a dark gray is easier on the eyes.
- Lighten accent colors a bit in dark mode so they keep enough contrast.

```js
function isPrime(n) {
  if (!Number.isInteger(n) || n < 2) return false;
  if (n === 2) return true;
  if (n % 2 === 0) return false;
  for (let i = 3; i * i <= n; i += 2) {
    if (n % i === 0) return false;
  }
  return true;
}

console.log(isPrime(2));   // true
console.log(isPrime(17));  // true
console.log(isPrime(21));  // false
console.log(isPrime(1));   // false
```

**How it works:**
- Numbers below 2 (and non-integers) aren't prime.
- 2 is the only even prime, so other even numbers are rejected right away.
- It only checks odd divisors up to **√n** — if `n` had a factor larger than √n, it would also have one smaller, which we'd already have found.

That makes it fast enough for numbers into the billions. For checking many numbers at once, a Sieve of Eratosthenes is better — want that version?
